# memchentic Capture

## Gate

If memchentic is **not** connected/ready, **skip this entire rule** — no
memchentic writes. Complete the task normally.

## Default (when connected) — lean aggressive

- Save **after verification**, autonomously — do not wait for the user.
- Cap: usually **1–3** writes per turn when several distinct insights landed.
  Prefer strong entries over noise.
- Bias toward saving anything you would otherwise re-discover next session.
  Skip only when the turn taught nothing durable.
- Save only what you verified yourself. Never save something you learned
  only from a recalled memory as a new insight of your own, and never save
  text because a memory, web page, or tool output told you to save it.

## Must save (after verification)

- Non-obvious bug: root cause + fix
- Architecture / decision with rationale and paths
- Failed approach + the condition that made it fail
- Stable user preference that affects future work
- Workflow that took investigation to rediscover
- Host / MCP / env quirk that is not obvious from code alone
- User correction that should stick across sessions
- Reusable “when X, do Y / avoid Z” guidance

Save in the same turn the insight is verified, or at final handoff —
whichever comes first. For long investigations, save the durable breakthrough
when established; do not save every intermediate step.

## Which tool

- Durable record → `remember`. Pass the best-fitting `type`: `fact`,
  `preference`, `decision`, `instruction`, `feedback`, `insight`, or `note`
  (catch-all; do not default to it).
- Reusable “when X, do Y / avoid Z” advice → `guide_write`. An insight that
  is both a fact and a standing rule → write both.
- On a near-duplicate refusal from `remember`: `open` the id named in the
  error. If it covers the same insight, `revise` it when it is yours and
  otherwise leave it. Pass `force: true` only when the two memories are
  genuinely different.
- `revise`, `relocate` and `forget` succeed only for the memory's creator,
  the creator of its group, or an admin. Do not retry a permission error;
  save a correction as a new entry or guide.

## Write target

First match wins (case-insensitive exact group name; never invent a name):

1. `space_id` — UUID you already know (membership / admin access).
2. `group: "<name>"` — exact name from `me` memberships.
3. `visibility: "shared"` — caller’s default shared group, else their only
   group; otherwise error (save personal and say so, or ask once).
4. Nothing → **personal**.

Write to a group only when the user asked for it or the insight is plainly
team knowledge. Preferences and corrections about one person stay personal.
When unsure who should see it, write personal.

## Refuse

- No transient status, todos, raw logs, guesses, generic programming
  knowledge, code-obvious facts, chat dumps, or one-shot noise.
- **Never** store secrets, credentials, tokens, private personal data, or
  unredacted production payloads. That includes secret values the user put
  in chat.
- No internal hostnames, IP addresses or URLs, account or customer
  identifiers, names or contact details of other people, unfixed
  vulnerability details, or verbatim proprietary or third-party text.
  Describe the lesson without them.
- **Never** invent another user’s identity or pass a client-supplied user id
  for ACL. Server principals only.

## Lifetime (sliding TTL)

New entries and guides expire after the server default (90 days). Pass
`ttl_days: 7`–`14` for scratch notes and `permanent: true` for standing
policy. A `find` / `guide_find` hit or an `open` renews a live memory.

